1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,144
This workshop joins six assigned reading rows rather than turning them
into unrelated facts. Follow one patient from the access decision
through delivery, monitoring and explanation, with the clinical-
judgment reading guiding repeated assessment.

2
00:00:15,144 --> 00:00:30,744
CPSC supplies injection and line-care principles. Cardiovascular
Pathophysiology explains the named rhythm mechanisms. VSM strengthens
the measurements that tell you whether the electrical and treatment
findings matter clinically.

3
00:00:30,744 --> 00:00:43,656
The saved examination IV and diabetic pages and current named BCEHS
procedures are taught separately where they differ. Do not blend a
remembered number from one source with the route or timing from
another.

4
00:00:43,656 --> 00:00:57,960
All eleven assigned Week Three reading rows now have paired
explanatory guides across this week's lessons. This does not certify
the unresolved school editions, missing source activities or the full
week's assessed requirements.

5
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:08,784
Start with the current care plan. If there is no indicated vascular
treatment or relevant guideline requirement, a technically easy vein
is not a reason to insert a catheter.

6
00:01:08,784 --> 00:01:22,536
PR twenty-six calls the three JAY questions Justifiable, Accountable
and You. Ask whether the need is defensible, whether peers would
support the care and how the patient experiences the risk and benefit.

7
00:01:22,536 --> 00:01:36,144
Consent remains necessary, but consent to an unnecessary procedure
does not by itself make the procedure clinically appropriate. Skill
maintenance belongs in the proper supervised education process.

8
00:01:36,144 --> 00:01:48,120
For the school station, state the indication, an alternative and a
reason to stop. An observable decision is more useful than silently
proceeding until you get a successful line.

9
00:01:48,120 --> 00:02:00,264
Aseptic continuity is the central link between the two assigned CPSC
chapters. Hand hygiene at the beginning does not protect a syringe tip
that later touches a dirty surface.

10
00:02:00,264 --> 00:02:12,984
Identify the key parts before you begin: the catheter connection,
tubing spike, distal connector and any access surface that leads into
the bloodstream. Handle the system so those parts stay protected.

11
00:02:12,984 --> 00:02:26,400
Use the actual antiseptic and connector instructions, including
drying. The archive example for one alcohol scrub and one positive-
displacement connector is not every product on a modern ambulance.

12
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:38,472
If you contaminate a critical component, pause and correct the break.
Do not conceal it, continue with tighter gloves or assume a flush will
wash the contamination harmlessly away.

13
00:02:38,472 --> 00:02:52,488
Priming fills the administration system with the intended fluid before
patient connection. First verify the fluid, expiry, clarity, bag
integrity and correct set rather than just choosing the nearest bag.

14
00:02:52,488 --> 00:03:05,544
Control the flow, protect the spike and bag port and connect without
contamination. Use the actual set instructions for chamber filling and
for clearing its ports, valves and complete length.

15
00:03:05,544 --> 00:03:18,384
Inspect the entire primed route, close the appropriate clamp and keep
the distal end protected. A set that falls onto a contaminated surface
is not made acceptable by running more fluid through it.

16
00:03:18,384 --> 00:03:30,888
Before connection, confirm the access and current plan. Priming a
disconnected air-containing system through a patient is not a
shortcut, and a pump cannot compensate for an improperly prepared
line.

17
00:03:30,888 --> 00:03:42,264
Trace the complete path from product to patient. The correct medicine
in a correctly labelled bag can still fail to arrive if a clamp is
closed or the secondary arrangement is wrong.

18
00:03:42,264 --> 00:03:55,680
For direct intravenous medication, include the volume between the port
and the patient. Medicine left in that space is prepared but not yet
delivered; the following flush can change its delivery rate.

19
00:03:55,680 --> 00:04:07,512
Read the actual device and drug plan for flushing and compatibility.
Do not improvise drug mixing in a prefilled flush syringe or treat a
sample tubing volume as universal.

20
00:04:07,512 --> 00:04:18,696
Record actual amounts, including partial delivery and relevant
residual. A pump screen or an empty syringe alone is not a complete
account of what entered the patient.

21
00:04:18,696 --> 00:04:30,672
This is an original arithmetic exercise, not a treatment order. The
simulation asks for one hundred twenty millilitres over forty minutes
through a set labelled twenty drops per millilitre.

22
00:04:30,672 --> 00:04:44,400
Multiply volume by drops per millilitre and divide by minutes.
Millilitres cancel, leaving sixty drops per minute. A pump-equivalent
calculation instead converts the forty minutes to hours.

23
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:57,432
The result is one hundred eighty millilitres per hour. If the actual
set changes, the gravity drops change even though the volume and time
do not. Read the package rather than guessing the drop factor.

24
00:04:57,432 --> 00:05:11,808
Both the archive and examination example contain a drop-factor unit
error. The factor is drops per millilitre, not drops per minute.
Correct arithmetic still requires checking actual delivery and
clinical response.

25
00:05:11,808 --> 00:05:22,704
A saline lock provides intermittent peripheral access. The word lock
is not permission to add heparin, and its presence does not prove the
line is still safe or required.

26
00:05:22,704 --> 00:05:35,784
Assess the site before access, prepare the actual connector and use
its appropriate flush plan. New pain, swelling, leakage or resistance
changes the plan before another medicine is given.

27
00:05:35,784 --> 00:05:49,272
Positive, negative and neutral displacement mechanisms are not
interchangeable. The correct clamp and disconnect sequence must come
from the device you are actually using, not a remembered Max Plus
photograph.

28
00:05:49,272 --> 00:06:02,664
Conversion to an infusion or back to a lock needs a current plan,
protected connections and patient reassessment. Reconnecting an
exposed contaminated line is not acceptable continuity of care.

29
00:06:02,664 --> 00:06:14,592
A patient can develop a local delivery failure and a separate systemic
problem at the same time. Inspecting the arm does not replace an
airway, breathing and circulation reassessment.

30
00:06:14,592 --> 00:06:28,464
Stop implicated administration when new concerning local findings
arise. Do not force a flush or increase pressure to overcome swelling.
Suspected extravasation requires the actual product-specific plan.

31
00:06:28,464 --> 00:06:43,008
The archive often says remove the cannula immediately. Some
extravasation plans instead preserve it initially for aspiration or
treatment. This is why a general rule must not replace the particular
product pathway.

32
00:06:43,008 --> 00:06:55,128
Explain the treatment timeline, exact product and amount, local
findings and patient deterioration when escalating. A silent pump is
not evidence that the therapy caused no harm.

33
00:06:55,128 --> 00:07:06,072
A short peripheral catheter and a PICC may both enter through an arm,
but their tips and risks differ. A PICC is central access despite its
peripheral entry site.

34
00:07:06,072 --> 00:07:19,344
Nontunnelled lines, tunnelled lines and implanted ports have different
connection and maintenance methods. Ask what the device is, why it is
present, what is infusing and whether there is damage or infection
concern.

35
00:07:19,344 --> 00:07:32,592
The assigned nursing reading covers these devices, but that does not
authorize a PCP to access an unfamiliar port or reposition a central
catheter. Protect it and obtain the appropriate trained support.

36
00:07:32,592 --> 00:07:45,624
A flow problem can be mechanical, thrombotic, positional or related to
the infusion. Do not blindly flush against resistance, pull a line
back or use scissors near an unfamiliar catheter.

37
00:07:45,624 --> 00:08:00,864
The school assigned the whole chapter domain, so blood therapy is not
silently omitted. Red cells carry oxygen, platelets support hemostasis
and plasma supplies clotting factors; saline is not a substitute for
those functions.

38
00:08:00,864 --> 00:08:13,416
Identity and compatibility must remain correct from sampling to the
bedside. A processing label such as irradiated or leukocyte reduced
does not make ABO-incompatible red cells safe.

39
00:08:13,416 --> 00:08:29,328
Learn to recognize a possible reaction and communicate the product and
timing. New fever, rigors, rash, pain, breathing difficulty or
hypotension must not be dismissed or followed by flushing suspect
blood into the patient.

40
00:08:29,328 --> 00:08:44,952
Actual transfusion requires its own trained authorized process and
product-specific equipment. This reading gives background recognition
and safety, not a new PCP transfusion qualification or an intravenous
epinephrine instruction.

41
00:08:44,952 --> 00:08:57,384
Parenteral nutrition is a prescribed nutrient formulation, not
ordinary maintenance saline. Its route, filter, dedicated-line
arrangement and monitoring are part of a specialist plan.

42
00:08:57,384 --> 00:09:12,000
Complications include glucose disturbance, fluid overload, line
problems and infection. Refeeding after substantial undernutrition can
produce dangerous phosphate, potassium, magnesium and water shifts.

43
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:26,424
Never increase the rate to catch up a missed amount. If nutrition is
interrupted, assess glucose, insulin exposure and the patient and
obtain the actual continuity plan instead of automatically
substituting another bag.

44
00:09:26,424 --> 00:09:39,384
The archive example of D five at the same rate is not a universal
paramedic instruction. Recognizing a harmful infusion may require
immediate interruption while appropriate replacement support is
arranged.

45
00:09:39,384 --> 00:09:52,896
The rhythm lesson introduced one repeatable method. Apply it again to
the broader chapter reading: patient first, then recording quality,
rate, regularity, atrial activity, conduction relationship and QRS.

46
00:09:52,896 --> 00:10:04,656
Look for evidence that could contradict your first label. An early P
hidden in a T wave, an independent atrial clock or artifact can change
the explanation of an apparent pause.

47
00:10:04,656 --> 00:10:16,968
Electrical rate is not a measurement of blood flow. The same tracing
can have different clinical urgency in a comfortable well-perfused
patient and someone who is hypotensive or losing consciousness.

48
00:10:16,968 --> 00:10:28,872
Describe what you can establish and what remains uncertain. A
provisional interpretation with its evidence is safer than a confident
name that ignores the patient or recording limitations.

49
00:10:28,872 --> 00:10:43,464
Atrial fibrillation has disorganized atrial activation without
consistent distinct sinus P waves. Its ordinary ventricular response
is irregularly irregular, but the ventricular rate need not always be
fast.

50
00:10:43,464 --> 00:10:58,944
Flutter is more organized. A stable two-to-one relationship can
produce a regular ventricular rhythm near one hundred fifty, while
variable conduction changes that pattern. Neither rate nor
irregularity alone proves flutter.

51
00:10:58,944 --> 00:11:12,840
Multifocal atrial tachycardia shows distinct P waves with at least
three shapes in one lead and an irregular tachycardic rhythm. Look for
those waves instead of calling every irregular tracing fibrillation.

52
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:26,640
Underlying pulmonary or systemic illness may be important. An
apparently benign rhythm label does not make a breathless patient
safe, and introductory recognition is not authority to trial an AV-
nodal medicine.

53
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:40,056
An atrial premature beat occurs before the next expected sinus
activation. Its P wave may differ or hide within the previous T wave,
and it may conduct normally, aberrantly or not at all.

54
00:11:40,056 --> 00:11:52,224
A blocked premature P can look like a pause if you only count QRS
complexes. Compare atrial timing and shape before deciding that the
sinus node stopped or an AV block developed.

55
00:11:52,224 --> 00:12:06,552
A ventricular premature beat usually has an early broad, different QRS
without a normally timed preceding P. A compensatory pause may follow,
but no single pause rule defines every ectopic pattern.

56
00:12:06,552 --> 00:12:19,992
Record the pattern and clinical context. One unusual complex does not
tell you the entire cause, and treating the tracing without
reassessing symptoms and perfusion misses the school assessment
objective.

57
00:12:19,992 --> 00:12:34,872
The original diagram separates atrial and ventricular timing. Orange
dots mark P onset; the grid represents standard speed and gain. These
are deliberately simplified teaching traces, not school examination
strips.

58
00:12:34,872 --> 00:12:47,688
In row A, each P is followed by a QRS, but the PR interval is point
two four seconds. At standard speed that is six small boxes, longer
than the usual adult point two zero boundary.

59
00:12:47,688 --> 00:13:01,992
This is first-degree conduction delay: the relevant atrial impulses
still conduct. It is not the same as an intermittently missing
ventricular response. Inspect the rest of the ECG and the patient as
well.

60
00:13:01,992 --> 00:13:13,824
Do not jump straight from long PR to a treatment. The clinical rate,
medicines, ischemic symptoms, perfusion and any evolving conduction
change determine why the finding matters.

61
00:13:13,824 --> 00:13:27,960
In diagram row B, PR lengthens across the conducted beats, then a P is
not followed by QRS and the sequence restarts. The missing event is
the expected ventricular response, not the atrial P.

62
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:40,296
Row C illustrates unchanged PR in the conducted beats with
intermittent nonconducted P waves. The full pattern and exclusions
matter when interpreting a possible Mobitz type two block.

63
00:13:40,296 --> 00:13:50,904
A two-to-one pattern alone does not tell you which type it is, because
you cannot see a complete sequence of progressive PR behavior. Do not
turn the ratio into certainty.

64
00:13:50,904 --> 00:14:04,536
A slow poorly perfusing patient needs urgent assessment and
appropriate support regardless of how elegant the label is. Pacing and
medicines still require their actual licence, training and service
pathway.

65
00:14:04,536 --> 00:14:16,728
In row D the atrial interval is point eight seconds and the
ventricular interval is one point five seconds. Their timing is
independent, so apparent PR relationships keep changing.

66
00:14:16,728 --> 00:14:28,752
Some P waves coincide with or hide in other complexes. Track the
atrial clock across the whole strip instead of declaring every nearby
P and QRS a conducted pair.

67
00:14:28,752 --> 00:14:43,224
Complete AV block means there is no effective atrioventricular
conduction. An escape pacemaker can continue ventricular activation,
but its origin determines the rate and width rather than one fixed
textbook number.

68
00:14:43,224 --> 00:14:55,320
Assess consciousness, pressure, pulse and symptoms immediately. A
visible escape rhythm is not a reason to assume sufficient cardiac
output or defer appropriate escalation.

69
00:14:55,320 --> 00:15:06,816
Bundle branch block delays activation of part of the ventricles. The
named morphology needs the appropriate lead pattern, not only a wide
complex or a remembered M shape.

70
00:15:06,816 --> 00:15:22,176
Pre-excitation uses an accessory atrioventricular pathway. A short PR
and slurred delta-wave onset can support recognition, but a manifest
pattern is not the same as every symptomatic WPW presentation.

71
00:15:22,176 --> 00:15:36,792
A broad tachycardia may be ventricular or have another abnormal
activation mechanism. If uncertain, recognize the risk, describe
evidence and obtain the appropriate care rather than testing a drug by
guesswork.

72
00:15:36,792 --> 00:15:49,608
Bundle block and pacing also alter ST and T appearance. New left
bundle block alone does not prove acute infarction, while a confounded
tracing does not exclude acute coronary disease.

73
00:15:49,608 --> 00:16:04,608
The reading connects ionic balance with electrical behavior. Calcium
changes may affect QT duration, and potassium disturbances may affect
atrial activity, conduction, ventricular activation and dangerous
rhythms.

74
00:16:04,608 --> 00:16:17,112
Do not memorize a tracing as an exact serum potassium concentration.
BCEHS explicitly notes that specific ECG changes do not reliably
correlate with specific potassium levels.

75
00:16:17,112 --> 00:16:30,744
Consider renal disease, missed dialysis, medicines, weakness and the
pace of deterioration. Describe what you see and why it raises
concern, without claiming the monitor has replaced laboratory testing.

76
00:16:30,744 --> 00:16:43,176
Recognition and escalation do not create authority to give advanced
electrolyte treatments. Continue patient support and the actual
consultation and conveyance plan appropriate to your role.

77
00:16:43,176 --> 00:16:57,432
A pulse oximeter estimates saturation, not the amount of hemoglobin or
blood flow. An anemic or poorly perfused patient can have an
apparently reassuring percentage and still inadequate oxygen delivery.

78
00:16:57,432 --> 00:17:09,720
Check the signal and actual sensor fit, movement and perfusion. A
displayed pulse that agrees with palpation supports signal validity
but does not guarantee saturation accuracy.

79
00:17:09,720 --> 00:17:25,248
Carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide are not interchangeable. Ordinary
oximetry cannot reliably separate carboxyhemoglobin from
oxyhemoglobin; hypercapnia is not the explanation for that carbon
monoxide limitation.

80
00:17:25,248 --> 00:17:37,992
If the patient looks worse than the number, respond to the patient
while checking the reading. Use the applicable oxygen plan rather than
adopting a broad textbook hospital range as every patient's target.

81
00:17:37,992 --> 00:17:50,472
Prepare the actual cuff and supported limb before inflation. The first
step palpates the pulse until it disappears; the candidate then adds
thirty millimetres of mercury to plan the listening inflation.

82
00:17:50,472 --> 00:18:03,744
Deflate completely and allow the actual taught recovery interval
before the second measurement. Listen over the brachial artery while
releasing evenly, around two millimetres per second in this candidate
method.

83
00:18:03,744 --> 00:18:16,920
The first sound gives systolic pressure and disappearance usually
gives adult diastolic pressure. A temporary silent gap can make a
later sound return look like systolic if you skipped the palpatory
estimate.

84
00:18:16,920 --> 00:18:29,856
Record the real method and conditions and connect the pressure to
perfusion and treatment response. Repeating a technically poor number
endlessly while the patient deteriorates is not reassessment.

85
00:18:29,856 --> 00:18:42,336
Altered awareness calls for patient assessment and glucose
measurement, not an automatic diabetes label. Airway support and other
immediate threats remain important while you identify the cause.

86
00:18:42,336 --> 00:18:58,680
Oral treatment requires a patient who can safely manage the route. If
vascular treatment is indicated, verify the line and product. If
access is not available, use the actual permitted alternative rather
than endless unsuccessful attempts.

87
00:18:58,680 --> 00:19:15,576
D ten contains point one gram per millilitre. For arithmetic only,
fifteen grams requires one hundred fifty millilitres. A sealed nasal
glucagon product and an injectable vial are different instructions,
not interchangeable concentrations.

88
00:19:15,576 --> 00:19:29,304
Recheck glucose and clinical response, protect the airway and consider
recurrence and the cause. A brief improvement does not automatically
settle capacity, disposition or the need for continued monitoring.

89
00:19:29,304 --> 00:19:40,080
This week contains real differences between sources. The school awake
intraosseous case and current PR twelve do not give the same adult
lidocaine amount and timing.

90
00:19:40,080 --> 00:19:53,472
The independent PCP IO branch is also restricted; two failed IV
attempts in an awake patient do not alone authorize it. A separate
branch requires direct on-scene advanced-provider supervision.

91
00:19:53,472 --> 00:20:06,096
Keep school learning requirements, the saved examination revision and
current operating guidance visible as different contexts. Ask for
instructor clarification where the actual assessment depends on the
version.

92
00:20:06,096 --> 00:20:18,672
Do not average times or borrow a route from one table and a dose from
another. A source-located unresolved difference is more honest and
useful than an invented apparently complete drug card.

93
00:20:18,672 --> 00:20:31,800
Use the original vomiting patient from the practical lesson. State why
the current plan needs access and what else could explain weakness and
low pressure. Do not assume all the problem is simple volume loss.

94
00:20:31,800 --> 00:20:44,352
During an authorized simulation infusion, the patient develops arm
pain and swelling and becomes more breathless. The pump still shows
its intended setting. What has actually changed?

95
00:20:44,352 --> 00:20:59,520
Stop implicated administration and assess ABCs, perfusion, the local
limb and the complete delivery path. Do not flush swelling away or
decide that finding an infiltration explains every new breathing
symptom.

96
00:20:59,520 --> 00:21:13,416
Use the school three questions: what changed, what should have
changed, and what got worse? Communicate the expected benefit, actual
product and amount, timing, observed harm and next care plan.

97
00:21:13,416 --> 00:21:53,400
Use the source pulse-force scale as documented: 3+ full/bounding, 2+
normal/strong, 1+ weak/diminished/thready and 0 absent or not
palpable. Do not silently switch it to a different four-plus scale.
Compare appropriate paired sites and describe a new asymmetry, but do
not convert a subjective force grade into a numeric blood pressure or
diagnosis. In the source respiratory sinus-arrhythmia pattern, rate
rises during inspiration and falls during expiration; it can occur in
young people, but not every irregular pulse has that mechanism.

98
00:21:53,400 --> 00:22:29,376
Prepare the actual listening instrument before interpreting silence.
Clean the contact surfaces and earpieces, point the earpieces forward
toward the nose, select the open chestpiece side and make a complete
skin seal. The source uses light pressure with a bell and firmer
contact with a diaphragm; follow the actual instrument design rather
than squeezing harder to force a sound. Korotkoff sounds arise during
partial cuff compression/deflation and are not the ECG, a heart sound
or proof that no circulation exists whenever the cuffed artery is
silent.

99
00:22:29,376 --> 00:23:11,256
Distinguish the assigned one-step comparison from skipping the safety
estimate. In the source two-step method, first palpate to
disappearance, add its 30-mmHg margin, fully deflate and then perform
the listening inflation/controlled deflation. Its one-step comparison
also estimates the disappearance point and margin, but proceeds to
listening during that inflation cycle instead of a separate full-
deflation cycle. The school emphasis this week is the two-step
demonstration; use its taught recovery/position/cuff procedure and do
not invent a fixed inflation of 200 mmHg for every arm.

100
00:23:11,256 --> 00:23:48,024
If Korotkoff sounds continue to zero, the assigned source uses
muffling as the alternative diastolic endpoint; record the endpoint
and what actually happened. If an auscultatory gap occurs, preserve
the true first systolic appearance and final diastolic endpoint and
note the temporary silence, rather than using the later return as a
new systolic value. These are sound-identification tasks. A printed
description and original rehearsal do not certify that a learner has
heard every example or completed the missing textbook audio/video
activities.

101
00:23:48,024 --> 00:24:31,032
Complete the older diabetic pathway as a sequence: manage
airway/breathing, obtain glucose and other baseline findings, consider
stroke or another cause, delegate egress preparation and choose a safe
permitted correction route. Its stated oxygen goal is at least 94
percent, and its low-glucose threshold is below 4.0 mmol/L. D10W is
both a carbohydrate substrate and an immediate glucose/water source;
the monograph also lists transient osmotic diuresis. Immediate IV
onset is not a promise of permanent recovery or a reason to omit a
repeat glucose and whole-patient reassessment.

102
00:24:31,032 --> 00:25:06,960
The actual older glucagon card lists onset 15 minutes, peak 15 minutes
and duration 90 minutes, with hypersensitivity as its stated
contraindication. Its caution list includes nausea/vomiting,
hypokalemia, urticaria, respiratory distress and hypotension. These
are source-specific entries to recognize, not a promise that every
patient follows that clock or develops each effect. Watch the patient
and airway, reconcile what was given and do not postpone needed
support while waiting for the listed onset.

103
00:25:06,960 --> 00:25:52,464
Keep the current nasal-product exclusions beside, not merged into,
that older IM/IN card. The checked BCEHS monograph excludes intranasal
use below age four and lists hypersensitivity, pheochromocytoma and
insulinoma. It supplies a sealed 3-mg nasal kit; its listed repeat is
after 15 minutes as needed, maximum 6 mg, with CliniCall required
before the second nasal dose. Current PCP selection requires confirmed
hypoglycemia, IV access unavailable and unsafe oral administration.
Limited hepatic glycogen can reduce benefit. None of this authorizes
making a nasal kit from an injectable product.

104
00:25:52,464 --> 00:26:30,576
Use a named screening tool for its intended question. The assigned
page names PHQ-9 for depressive symptoms, GAD-7 for anxiety symptoms
and C-SSRS for suicide-risk assessment. These do not replace the
interview, mental-status examination, physical observations or
immediate safety assessment. Record the tool and context if it is
actually used; a score alone neither establishes a diagnosis nor
determines capacity, disposition or legal authority. A calm interview
does not clear an abnormal glucose, falling pressure or new fever.

105
00:26:30,576 --> 00:27:09,096
Turn the ethical-strategies table into observable conduct: protect
privacy and unnecessary bodily exposure, reduce environmental stress
and ask what has helped the person regain control before. Keep any
provider self-disclosure brief, purposeful and directed back to the
person rather than seeking friendship. After a distressing event,
support explanation, listening, debrief and appropriate team or ethics
assistance. The source U.S. nursing restraint, seclusion and
medication examples do not supply BC paramedic authority or a
universal sequence.

106
00:27:09,096 --> 00:27:48,720
An original education example makes PICOT concrete. P is student
paramedics in an IV simulation; I is a structured line-and-site check
prompt; C is usual simulation teaching; O is the proportion completing
all specified checks correctly; T is the end of one training session.
This is a proposed research question, not evidence that the prompt
works. Define the observations, fair comparison and safety safeguards
before drawing a conclusion. A decision tree instead organizes
conditional actions, such as whether help is available; it does not
test this teaching effect.

107
00:27:48,720 --> 00:28:29,352
Read the source examination discussion as context, not an AET
blueprint. Its eight broad nursing categories cover care management,
medication/parenteral therapy, physiological adaptation, risk
reduction, safety/infection, health promotion, psychosocial integrity
and basic care/comfort. Its bow-tie, matrix, highlight, trend and
other interactive examples change how information is presented, not
the need to explain a cue, action and response. The source examination
date, time allowance and percentage chart are not this school's weekly
examination requirements.

108
00:28:29,352 --> 00:29:05,472
After antipsychotic exposure, a new combination of high fever,
muscular rigidity, altered awareness and unstable autonomic
observations raises concern for neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Other
dangerous causes remain possible. Support immediate physiological
needs and obtain urgent clinical help rather than waiting for a
complete laboratory pattern. The checked risperidone label calls for
immediate discontinuation when NMS is suspected, with treatment and
monitoring; this is not permission for a student to independently
change or later restart a prescription.

109
00:29:05,472 --> 00:29:42,432
The final assigned page also contains a recovery story about stopping
medication because of unwanted sexual effects, despite financial and
access support. The teaching point is to ask privately and without
blame what is difficult about the actual treatment, not to assume cost
is the only barrier or call the person noncompliant. Clarify the
medicine, timing, effects, priorities and immediate safety needs, then
support discussion with the treating prescriber. Long-term shared
medication review and emergency response to a suspected dangerous
reaction are different situations.

110
00:29:42,432 --> 00:30:21,720
Original reassessment rehearsal: a person taking an antipsychotic is
frightened and sweating. After respectful explanation they speak more
comfortably, but temperature rises and rigidity appears. Answer What
Else: consider a serious medication reaction and other medical causes,
verify actual medicines and timing, and reassess physiology without
delaying urgent support. What changed is the conversation; what should
also improve is physiological stability; what got worse is temperature
and muscle findings. Hand over both trajectories rather than
documenting merely calmer.

111
00:30:21,720 --> 00:31:02,520
The literal opening page completes nasal medication after the
administration step: hand hygiene, a record of the actual dose and
naris or nares, and the patient response. Across the assigned routes,
use the same chain of indication, identity, allergy, product, dose,
route, timing, explanation and reassessment. The book uses seven
rights and three label checks, including a final check before
delivery. These checks support, rather than replace, investigating an
unclear order or a patient concern. Do not continue an unidentified
preparation merely because a checklist was started.

112
00:31:02,520 --> 00:31:47,856
For a small-volume nebulizer, confirm the actual medication, diluent
and compatible device; assemble a protected cup, gas connection and
suitable mask or mouthpiece. Upright positioning when tolerated and a
mouth seal support delivery. Check that aerosol is actually produced
and monitor breathing, pulse, benefit and adverse effects. The source
gas-flow and eight-to-ten-minute examples are not every device or
treatment endpoint. Do not stop needed oxygen just to attach a
nebulizer or substitute oxygen and compressed air without considering
the oxygen plan. Clean, dry and store the device as instructed; rinse
after inhaled corticosteroid use.

113
00:31:47,856 --> 00:32:38,352
For a pressurized MDI, inspect the product and remaining doses, remove
the cover and prepare or prime only as that inhaler requires. With an
appropriate holding chamber, make a seal, release one puff, then
inhale slowly and hold briefly if able; without a chamber, actuation
must coordinate with a slow inhalation. Teach by demonstration and
return-demonstration rather than asking only whether the person
understands. The source conflicts with itself: one box says one-to-two
minutes between puffs, another says twenty-to-thirty seconds for one
medicine and two-to-five minutes for multiple medicines. The checked
Canadian Lung Association example uses one minute before a second
puff; the actual prescribed product and service plan control, not a
blended interval.

114
00:32:38,352 --> 00:33:19,920
A dry-powder inhaler is not the same breathing task. Load the actual
device correctly, keep it dry, breathe out away from it, seal the
mouthpiece and inhale with the device-required quick deep breath. Some
devices use a capsule that is loaded and pierced in the inhaler; that
does not turn the capsule into an oral dose. Do not exhale moisture
into the device or add an MDI spacer to a DPI. Check the dose
indicator or supplied dose-tracking method and observe technique. A
patient unable to generate the required inhalation needs reassessment
and an appropriate alternative, not repeated unsuccessful attempts
while deteriorating.

115
00:33:19,920 --> 00:34:16,368
For a topical or transdermal product, avoid exposing your own skin.
Reconcile all existing patches, the actual drug and application times;
remove the intended old patch as the prescribed replacement plan
requires, inspect the skin and select an appropriate clean dry new
site. The source includes hair clipping rather than shaving,
documentation and checking adhesion. Its ten-second pressure, one-week
site interval and nitroglycerin patch-free example are product-context
examples, not every patch schedule. Do not adopt its instruction to
cut a used patch for disposal: for the checked fentanyl product, keep
it intact, fold adhesive sides together and use the applicable
Canadian pharmacy or service disposal process. Avoid external heat and
secure it from accidental contact; removal does not make continuing
opioid toxicity impossible.

116
00:34:16,368 --> 00:34:55,392
The assigned intradermal tail illustrates a shallow bevel-up approach
at five-to-fifteen degrees and a small wheal in the dermis, followed
by needle safety, no massage, a site record and appropriate follow-up.
It is a different tissue target from SC or IM delivery, not a new PCP
tuberculosis-testing authorization. The displayed bevel and wheal help
explain the target; a photograph alone does not prove the learner can
place or interpret a test. Use the actual test-specific administration
and reading process rather than treating the book's three-day reminder
as a universal time for every skin test.

117
00:34:55,392 --> 00:35:44,928
SC delivery targets fatty tissue below the dermis. The source shows
upper-arm, abdominal, thigh and upper-gluteal examples, with forty-
five or ninety degrees selected according to tissue and needle; avoid
bruised, hardened, inflamed or otherwise unsuitable skin. The actual
insulin concentration, units, syringe or pen scale and authorized dose
must match. One pen belongs to one person even when its needle is
changed. A source list mentioning SC epinephrine does not change the
taught current anaphylaxis IM route. Do not copy the source phrase
within one inch of the umbilicus as insulin-site advice: the checked
Diabetes Canada teaching keeps abdominal insulin injections five
centimetres away.

118
00:35:44,928 --> 00:36:32,952
Correct the insulin table rather than memorizing its contradictions.
Rotate injection points to limit fatty lumps and variable absorption;
the sentence saying rotation is no longer necessary conflicts with its
own next instruction and current Diabetes Canada teaching. Insulin
types differ in meal relation, action and whether mixing is permitted;
an example sliding scale, ten-to-fifteen-minute meal timing or thirty-
day opened-vial life is not universal. Identify the actual product and
plan, including the last dose and food intake. Fruity breath is a
ketoacidosis warning, not a reliable sign of hypoglycemia. Measure
glucose and assess the whole patient; do not independently change
insulin because of the smell alone.

119
00:36:32,952 --> 00:37:23,856
The heparin table teaches a high-alert anticoagulant: identify the
actual UFH or LMWH product, dose and renal/bleeding context and report
new bleeding or suspected harm. Do not assume one aPTT rule applies to
every SC heparin; the checked Thrombosis Canada comparison separates
laboratory-adjusted IV UFH from the usual LMWH approach. Do not
massage an injection site as a way to improve absorption. An
indwelling SC device remains an SC route, not IV access. Its
indication, medication, site assessment, dressing, dead space and
change interval need a specific plan; source seven-day, fourteen-day
or fluid-volume examples do not authorize universal extra dosing or a
fixed replacement clock.

120
00:37:23,856 --> 00:38:13,896
Read the three IM landmarks anatomically. Deltoid lies below the
acromion in the appropriate central muscle area, not at the shoulder
joint; vastus lateralis uses the middle anterolateral thigh; the
source ventrogluteal method relates the greater trochanter, anterior
superior iliac spine and iliac crest to a muscle triangle. Expose and
identify the actual landmarks rather than guessing through clothing or
applying one hand size to everyone. Choose the permitted site and
needle for age, tissue depth, muscle, product and volume. The source
preference for ventrogluteal injection is not a mandate to use it for
all vaccines, ages or emergency medicines, nor a guarantee that no
nearby vessel or nerve exists.

121
00:38:13,896 --> 00:39:04,320
The source Z-track sequence displaces skin sideways, maintains that
displacement during a ninety-degree IM injection, withdraws the needle
and then releases the skin to offset the track. It also teaches clean
dry skin, stabilization, prompt sharps safety, no recapping,
documentation and response review. Its displacement distances and ten-
seconds-per-millilitre or ten-second dwell are source examples, not
every medicine or injector instruction. A higher gauge number is a
smaller bore, not a shortcut to a deeper muscle. No blood on
aspiration does not prove correct placement; current CDC vaccine
guidance does not require aspiration at recommended vaccination sites.
Other medications still require their actual trained product/service
method.

122
00:39:04,320 --> 00:40:03,312
For direct IV medication, identify which of the three assigned
situations exists: a locked line, a running compatible infusion or a
running incompatible infusion. Verify permission to interrupt the
existing therapy; do not commandeer a critical insulin, heparin, blood
or nutrition route merely because it is connected. Trace the actual
fluid path and control the source flow without closing the medication-
to-patient path while injecting; do not blindly copy ambiguous distal-
clamp wording. Use the drug-specific concentration and rate,
compatible flush and actual residual volume. The source twenty-
millilitre maximum, at-least-one-minute rule, ten-millilitre syringe
examples and claim that no medicine stays in tubing are not universal.
Its final page completes administration, resumed prescribed flow,
reassessment and documentation, not all Chapter 8.

123
00:40:03,312 --> 00:40:50,952
Keep the actual D03 reference beside the clinical judgment: its
general PCP line uses normal saline in up-to-twenty-millilitre-per-
kilogram increments, reassessing every five hundred millilitres and
after the bolus, including pressure and pulmonary edema. Its general
systolic target is at least ninety, with different trauma targets and
consultation before exceeding two litres. Its separate child line uses
five-to-ten-millilitre-per-kilogram increments, maximum twenty,
without removing the under-twelve PCP cannulation prohibition. For
peripheral attempts, it says generally two per practitioner but to
reconsider need after two total failures. Neither rule licenses
endless new attempts by rotating providers.

124
00:40:50,952 --> 00:41:38,856
PR12 names recent use of the same site within forty-eight hours, local
infection, relevant tissue or skeletal injury and previous proximal
tibial surgery or knee replacement as exclusions. Protect CPR, airway
care and defibrillation throughout preparation; the three-round
compression/AED-analysis statement appears in the ACP section, not
permission to delay resuscitation for an IO. Its usual catheter
duration is up to twenty-four hours, with an exceptional extension up
to forty-eight hours in patients over twelve, not a routine two-day
transport plan. Repeated limb/site assessment and handover remain
necessary; the school/current awake-lidocaine timing difference stays
separately labelled.

125
00:41:38,856 --> 00:42:33,768
The LP15 reading gives a usable device sequence: prepare leads and
skin, place electrodes, select 12 Lead and confirm age and sex using
the scroll control. With noise, correct movement, contact or cable
tension; prolonged noise can cancel acquisition and requires
restarting. Forcing acceptance despite persistent noise is at ACP
discretion, not an automatic PCP shortcut. For transmission, Options
then Patient supplies identifiers and the pain-onset field; Transmit
selects the record and destination, then Send. Options then Print
selects an existing record for another print. Verify the actual record
and delivery status. These LP15 controls are not invented ZOLL
controls, and no real patient identifiers are entered in this course
rehearsal.

126
00:42:33,768 --> 00:43:19,392
For ZOLL, enter accurate demographics before acquisition so that the
ECG carries them to EPOS or the pPCI destination. The named page
distinguishes a STEMI flag from an ACUTE MI-only message, and its
additional-view sections are ACP-only; follow the actual pathway and
required consultation rather than a guessed button sequence. Both
device pages retain a ten-minute-after-sustained-ROSC recording note.
That specific post-arrest note is not the school three-minute
acquisition exercise, a pause in monitoring or a reason to withhold
immediate resuscitation. Record the patient timeline and continue
necessary care while applying the current post-arrest and consultation
plan.

127
00:43:19,392 --> 00:44:12,576
Understand additional views without changing the provider label. The
source posterior recording reuses V4/V5/V6 wires as V7 at the
posterior axillary line, V8 near the scapular tip and V9 paraspinally,
all at the appropriate V6 horizontal level, and relabels the printed
trace. A right-sided recording moves V4 to the right fifth intercostal
midclavicular site and labels it V4R. Lewis recording moves RA to the
right second and LA to the right fourth intercostal parasternal sites,
then reads Lead I. These are altered electrode configurations, not new
standard-lead labels to leave unmarked. The ZOLL page even retains a
copied LP15 printing sentence: do not infer actual ZOLL controls from
that sentence.

128
00:44:12,576 --> 00:44:52,080
Literal export page seventy-eight is PR27 iGel pharyngeal suction, not
more PR26. It addresses retained secretions and an impaired seal, not
PCP gastric intubation. First consider size, depth, positioning and
obstruction. Active vomiting, or difficult ventilation after active
vomiting, calls for removal, oropharyngeal suction and replacement as
required rather than passing the catheter deeper through the device.
Maintain ventilation as the priority. This explains the literal
reading; it does not add a new assessed Week Three station.

129
00:44:52,080 --> 00:45:29,760
For the PR27 setup, use the specified package-length guide plus about
two centimetres to mark the suction-catheter depth, lubricate the
channel and stop at that mark. Observe fluid removal and stop suction
once cleared, or after fifteen-to-twenty seconds without fluid; do not
leave continuous suction. Its stated pressure starts at eighty
millimetres of mercury and generally does not exceed one hundred
sixty. If the iGel dislodges, do not reinsert it with the suction tip
projecting beyond the device. Check actual equipment and reassess
ventilation.

130
00:45:29,760 --> 00:45:41,256
Have a partner watch the entire route preparation, not just the final
connection. Name the key parts and ask the partner to identify any
contamination or unverified transition.

131
00:45:41,256 --> 00:45:54,360
Explain one original conduction diagram without memorizing its
caption. Count atrial and ventricular events separately, describe the
relationship and say what patient findings could change urgency.

132
00:45:54,360 --> 00:46:06,624
Demonstrate a real two-step pressure and calculate a different actual-
set rate with full units. Explain the difference between a measured
number, an estimated value and a clinical conclusion.

133
00:46:06,624 --> 00:46:19,704
Close the simulation with a concise handover and record of reasoning
and response. This is preparation for the school stations, not a
signed competency, recovered school quiz or completed assessed
submission.
